  "prediction": "The Office of Personnel Management (OPM) reports that there are approximately 2.1 million civilian workers in the federal workforce. However, the size and scope of this workforce can vary depending on the data source and methodology used to calculate it. Some sources use headcounts, hours worked, surveys, or self-identification to determine the number of federal employees.\n\nTwo common methodologies used to measure federal employment are full-time equivalents (FTEs) and on-board personnel. FTEs are calculated based on the total number of hours worked and are used by the Office of Management and Budget (OMB) to manage employment levels. On-board personnel, on the other hand, refer to the actual number of employees, including full-time, part-time, and temporary workers. OPM uses this methodology to provide employee headcounts.\n\nThe differences in methodologies and data collection frequency can result in significant variations in the reported size of the federal workforce. For example, using FTEs instead of on-board personnel can result in a lower reported number of employees.\n\nOPM is the central human resources department of the executive branch and maintains a comprehensive database of federal civilian employment data called the Enterprise Human Resources Integration Data Mart (EHRD). This database covers approximately 96% of non-postal federal civilian employees and includes over 100 data elements. The data is available to the public through FedScope, a website that provides access to employment data, trends, and diversity statistics.\n\nThe President's Budget also includes federal employment statistics, which are calculated using the FTE methodology. The Budget provides detailed employment data by department, agency, and program, and is available online.\n\nUsers should be aware of the differences in methodologies and data collection frequency when using federal workforce statistics from various sources.",
  "scored_prediction": "The Office of Personnel Management (OPM) reports that there are approximately 2.1 million civilian workers in the federal workforce. However, the size and scope of this workforce can vary depending on the data source and methodology used to calculate it. Some sources use headcounts, hours worked, surveys, or self-identification to determine the number of federal employees.\n\nTwo common methodologies used to measure federal employment are full-time equivalents (FTEs) and on-board personnel. FTEs are calculated based on the total number of hours worked and are used by the Office of Management and Budget (OMB) to manage employment levels. On-board personnel, on the other hand, refer to the actual number of employees, including full-time, part-time, and temporary workers. OPM uses this methodology to provide employee headcounts.\n\nThe differences in methodologies and data collection frequency can result in significant variations in the reported size of the federal workforce. For example, using FTEs instead of on-board personnel can result in a lower reported number of employees.\n\nOPM is the central human resources department of the executive branch and maintains a comprehensive database of federal civilian employment data called the Enterprise Human Resources Integration Data Mart (EHRD). This database covers approximately 96% of non-postal federal civilian employees and includes over 100 data elements. The data is available to the public through FedScope, a website that provides access to employment data, trends, and diversity statistics.\n\nThe President's Budget also includes federal employment statistics, which are calculated using the FTE methodology. The Budget provides detailed employment data by department, agency, and program, and is available online.\n\nUsers should be aware of the differences in methodologies and data collection frequency when using federal workforce statistics from various sources.",
  "score": 22.119815244919202,
  "answers": [
    "This report describes online tools, reports, and data compilations created by the Office of Management and Budget (OMB) and the Office of Personnel Management (OPM) that contain statistics about federal employees and the federal workforce. The report also describes key characteristics of each resource and briefly discusses selected methodological differences, with the intention of facilitating the selection of appropriate data for specific purposes. This report is not intended to be a definitive list of all information on the federal workforce. It describes significant and recurring products that contain specific data often requested by Members or congressional staff."
  ],
